.TH PTY_RUN 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_run, pty_setenv, pty_signal \- start and control the program under test
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "int pty_run(PtySession *" s ", const char *" argv "[]);"
.BI "int pty_setenv(PtySession *" s ", const char *" name ", const char *" value );
.BI "int pty_signal(PtySession *" s ", int " sig );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_run ()
creates a pseudo-terminal of the session's size, forks, and executes
.IR argv [0]
(looked up in
.BR PATH )
with the slave side as its controlling terminal and its standard input,
output and error.
.I argv
is NULL-terminated.
A session runs at most one program.
.PP
The child starts with an empty signal mask and the default dispositions
for
.BR SIGPIPE ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGCHLD
and
.BR SIGALRM ,
whatever the test process has set.
Its environment is the test process's, with these changes, in order:
.IP 1. 4
.B TERM=xterm\-256color
and
.BR LC_ALL=en_US.UTF\-8 ;
.IP 2. 4
.B COLUMNS
and
.B LINES
removed, so the program asks the terminal for its size;
.IP 3. 4
every
.BR pty_setenv ()
of the session, in the order made.
.PP
.BR pty_setenv ()
records a variable to set in the child, or, when
.I value
is NULL, to remove from it.
A later call for the same name replaces the earlier one.
It affects only a later
.BR pty_run ();
the test process's own environment is not changed.
Isolate the program from the developer's files by setting at least
.BR HOME ,
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
and
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME .
.PP
.BR pty_signal ()
sends
.I sig
to the child.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR pty_run ()
returns 0 once the program has been executed.
It returns \-1 and sets
.I errno
if the arguments are invalid or a program already runs
.RB ( EINVAL ),
if the PTY or the process could not be created, or if
.BR execvp (3)
failed \(em in which case
.I errno
is the exec error, such as
.B ENOENT
for a missing program.
The exec result is reported through a close-on-exec pipe, so a missing
binary fails here rather than later as a timeout.
.PP
.BR pty_setenv ()
returns 0, or \-1 for a NULL session, an empty name, a name containing
\(oq=\(cq, or an allocation failure.
.PP
.BR pty_signal ()
returns 0, or \-1 if there is no child or
.BR kill (2)
failed.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
PtySession *s = pty_open(80, 24);
pty_setenv(s, "HOME", fixture_home);
pty_setenv(s, "XDG_CONFIG_HOME", NULL);
pty_setenv(s, "XDG_DATA_HOME", NULL);
pty_setenv(s, "XDG_CACHE_HOME", NULL);
const char *argv[] = { "./my\-tui", "\-\-verbose", NULL };
if (pty_run(s, argv) != 0) {
    printf("cannot start ./my\-tui: %s\en", strerror(errno));
    /* fail the test */
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_open (3),
.BR pty_wait_exit (3),
.BR forkpty (3),
.BR libptytest (7)
